  "notes": "The 1987 text is a prepared address. The 1975 speech’s quoted description of socialist ownership is not a general economic prescription. Its praise of avoiding terrorist executions does not establish abolition of every exceptional capital offense. Diaspora immigration support is specific to Jewish return, not unrestricted immigration. Later reported remarks receive medium confidence at most. The 1967 departure account is disputed and does not establish unqualified endorsement of forced expulsion. Pardons and commutations are recorded separately from ordinary capital punishment; the security-service pardons carried retirement conditions. Electoral reform concerned the prime minister and constituency representation, not direct election of the head of state. Party affiliation and business employment supply no economic-policy answers.\n\nMerged with an independent claude-sonnet-5-5 audit: 1 answers taken from it, 6 agreed, 0 conflicts settled by the integrator.\n\nclaude-sonnet-5-5 notes: Independent second pass. His memoirs and books (Living History, Who Stands Accused?, The Arab-Israeli Wars, Heroes of Israel) exist on archive.org only as restricted lending copies with no full text, so they could not be searched. Searched: UN Security Council verbatim records of 1976 (S/PV.1899, 1939, 1967; S/PV.1940 downloaded but not used), reports of his presidency and 1993 talk. Not found: Knesset 1981-83 speeches, 1987 Bundestag text, UN General Assembly records. Government and economy themes have no recorded position. Speeches at the UN were delivered for the Israeli government, so answers rest on points he argued himself at length. Electoral reform (1993 farewell speech) is left null because the report names no mechanism. Political violence is left null: he condemned terrorism but never addressed whether political violence could ever be justified.",
